; 16-expect.kurt

; So far, whenever a lesson wanted to say "this would fail", it could only
; describe it in a comment and ask you to try it yourself (see 05-const.kurt,
; 06-var.kurt).  `expect` lets a file demonstrate a mistake directly, and
; have Kurt itself confirm it still fails the way the lesson says it does:
; `expect "KIND"` opens a block whose content must raise an error of the
; given kind (`ScanError` a symbol can't be scanned, `ParseError` the line
; can't be read, `EvalError` it is readable but not allowed, `TypeError`,
; `ProofError` a claim can't be derived) -- if it does, the block (and everything inside it) is simply
; discarded and checking continues from the next line; if it doesn't, *that*
; is now the failure.

const zero

expect "EvalError"
    var zero          ; `zero` is already a constant -- this really does fail

true                  ; ...and we're back to normal, checking continues here

; `expect "KIND"` only checks the *kind* of error.  An optional second
; string, `expect "KIND" "TEXT"`, also checks the wording: the error message
; must contain TEXT.  A short, telling piece of the message is enough:

expect "EvalError" "already"
    var zero          ; the message says that `zero` is already a constant

; The error may come from anywhere inside the block, also from inside a
; nested block, or from a nested block that can't be closed:

bool A, B
use A
expect "ProofError"
    show B
    proof
        A             ; `B` doesn't follow, so the proof can't be closed
    qed
